utive columns, the right side of \\(R\\) is a vertical line at some column \\(c\\). If \\(R\\) contains two distinct squares \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) and \\((j,\\ell_j)\\) with \\(\\ell_i\\neq\\ell_j\\), then its right side must be at column \\(\\max\\{\\ell_i,\\ell_j\\}\\). Suppose \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_j\\). Then column \\(\\ell_j\\) belongs to \\(R\\); however row \\(i\\) has no square in column \\(\\ell_j\\) (its left part ends at \\(\\ell_i\\)). Hence \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) cannot belong to \\(R\\). Therefore **no rectangle can contain two different squares \\((i,\\ell_i)\\)**.  \n\nConsequently each non‑zero \\(\\ell_i\\) forces a distinct rectangle to cover its right‑most square. Exactly one row has \\(\\ell_i=0\\) (the row where \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\)). Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(L\\).\n\nThe same argument applied to the right part  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\; \\sigma(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ngives another lower bound of \\(n-1\\) rectangles, because the squares \\((i,n-r_i)\\) are all distinct and each rectangle can contain at most one of them.  \n\nAdding the two bounds yields the desired lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{R}}_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).  \n\nThe row \\(i=1\\) has \\(\\ell_1=0\\) and therefore receives no left‑hand tile; the row \\(i=n\\) has \\(r_n=0\\) and receives no right‑hand tile.  \nThus the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left ti