"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have solved the problem completely. The minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,2025\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,2025\\).\n\n2. **Notation for left/right parts of a row.**  \n   For a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:=\\sigma(i)-1\\qquad(\\text{number of squares left of the uncovered one}),\n   \\qquad\n   r_i:=2025-\\sigma(i)\\qquad(\\text{number of squares right of it}).\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(\\ell_i+r_i=2024\\) for every \\(i\\).  \n   Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,2025\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,2024\\}\\); the same holds for \\(\\{r_i\\}\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1.* Any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2\\,(2025-1)=4048\\) rectangles.  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Consider the **left part** of the board  \n\n   \\[\n   L:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le2025,\\;1\\le j\\le\\ell_i\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each row with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) the square \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) is the **right\u2011most** square of that row\u2019s left part.  \n   Since the numbers \\(\\ell_i\\) are all distinct, these right\u2011most squares lie in distinct columns.  \n\n   Let a rectangle \\(R\\) of the tiling intersect \\(L\\). Because a rectangle is a Cartesian product of a set of consecutive rows and a set of consecutive columns, its right side is a vertical line at some column \\(c\\).  \n   If \\(R\\) contained two different right\u2011most squares \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) and \\((j,\\ell_j)\\) with \\(\\ell_i\\neq\\ell_j\\), then without loss of generality \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_j\\). The column interval of \\(R\\) would have to contain \\(\\ell_j\\); consequently it would also contain column \\(\\ell_i+1=\\sigma(i)\\) in row \\(i\\). That square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is uncovered, contradicting the fact that a tile cannot cover an uncovered square. Hence **no rectangle can contain two distinct right\u2011most squares of \\(L\\)**.  \n\n   Exactly one row has \\(\\ell_i=0\\) (the row where \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\)). Therefore at least \\(2025-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all squares of \\(L\\).\n\n   The same argument applied to the **right part**  \n\n   \\[\n   R:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le2025,\\;\\sigma(i)+1\\le j\\le2025\\}\n   \\]\n\n   shows that at least another \\(2025-1\\) rectangles are required: the squares \\((i,2025-r_i)\\) (the left\u2011most squares of each right part) are all in distinct columns and cannot share a rectangle.  \n\n   Moreover a rectangle that contains a right\u2011most square of \\(L\\) cannot also contain a left\u2011most square of \\(R\\), because that would force the rectangle to cover an uncovered square as argued above. Consequently the two lower bounds add up, giving  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{rectangles}\\ge (2025-1)+(2025-1)=4048.\n   \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n\n   * Left tiles: for each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     T^{\\text{L}}_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n     \\]\n\n     which covers the left part of that row.  \n\n   * Right tiles: for each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<2025\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     T^{\\text{R}}_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le2025\\},\n     \\]\n\n     which covers the right part of that row.  \n\n   The first row has no left tile, and the last row has no right tile. Hence we obtain exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (2025-1)+(2025-1)=4048\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.  \n   The tiles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct rows, and in each row the left and right tile are separated by the uncovered diagonal square). Every unit square not on the diagonal belongs to exactly one tile, and each row and each column contains precisely its diagonal square as the unique uncovered cell.  \n\n   Thus the construction satisfies all conditions with \\(4048\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives a universal lower bound of \\(4048\\) tiles, and the explicit construction achieves this bound. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\},\\qquad n=2025 .\n\\]\n\nA *tiling* is a collection \\(\\mathcal{T}\\) of axis\u2011parallel rectangles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint and whose union is a subset of \\(B\\).  \nThe requirement \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 forces the set of uncovered squares to be  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=\\sigma(i)-1,\\qquad r_i:=n-\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_i\\) (resp. \\(r_i\\)) counts the squares to the left (resp. right) of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection,  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\{r_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Lower bound)\n\n*Statement.* Any tiling satisfying the condition uses at least \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine the left part of the board  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le\\ell_i\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\ell_i>0\\) the square \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) is the right\u2011most element of row \\(i\\) inside \\(L\\).  \nBy (1) the columns \\(\\ell_i\\) are all distinct, so these squares lie in distinct columns.\n\nConsider a rectangle \\(R\\in\\mathcal{T}\\) that meets \\(L\\).  \nLet its column interval be \\([c_{\\min},c_{\\max}]\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) contained two distinct right\u2011most squares \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) and \\((j,\\ell_j)\\) with \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_j\\), then \\(c_{\\max}\\ge\\ell_j\\). Consequently column \\(\\ell_i+1=\\sigma(i)\\) would also belong to the interval, and the square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) (which is uncovered) would be covered by \\(R\\) \u2013 impossible. Hence **each rectangle can contain at most one right\u2011most square of \\(L\\)**.\n\nExactly one row has \\(\\ell_i=0\\); all other \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(\\ell_i>0\\). Therefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all squares of \\(L\\).\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the right part  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nshows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required: the squares \\((i,n-r_i)\\) (the left\u2011most squares of each right part) are all in distinct columns and cannot share a rectangle.\n\nFinally, a rectangle that contains a right\u2011most square of \\(L\\) cannot also contain a left\u2011most square of \\(R\\); otherwise its column interval would contain both \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)\\) for some rows \\(i,j\\), forcing it to cover an uncovered square. Hence the two collections of rectangles are disjoint, and the lower bounds add:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{T}|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand for each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{R}}_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe first row has no left tile, the last row has no right tile.  \nThus the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)=4048 .\n\\]\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint (they occupy distinct rows, and in each row the left and right tile are separated by the uncovered diagonal square). Every unit square not on the diagonal belongs to exactly one tile, while the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered. Consequently each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Minimality\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that no admissible tiling can use fewer than \\(4048\\) rectangles, and the construction above uses exactly \\(4048\\) rectangles. Hence the lower bound is tight, and the minimal possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
