n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound \\(f(n)\\ge 2n-2\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that any tiling satisfying the conditions uses at\nleast \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n**Base case \\(n=1\\).**  The board consists of a single square, which must be the\nuncovered one; no tile is needed, so \\(f(1)=0=2\\cdot1-2\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  Assume the claim holds for size \\(n-1\\) (\\(n\\ge 2\\)).\nConsider an arbitrary tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board with uncovered squares\n\\(U=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\}\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nr:=\\sigma^{-1}(n)\\qquad(\\text{the unique row whose uncovered square lies in column }n).\n\\]\n\nThus the hole in column \\(n\\) is at \\((r,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** *Row \\(r\\) requires at least one rectangle, and column \\(n\\) requires at\nleast one rectangle; if \\(1<r<n\\) column \\(n\\) actually needs two rectangles.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nRow \\(r\\) contains all squares \\((r,c)\\) with \\(c\\neq n\\); they form a single\ncontiguous interval of columns \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Any rectangle covering any\nsquare of this row cannot contain column \\(n\\) (otherwise it would contain the hole\n\\((r,n)\\)), therefore the whole row‑segment must be covered by a rectangle whose\nrightmost column is at most \\(n-1\\).  Consequently at least one rectangle is needed\nfor row \\(r\\).\n\nColumn \\(n\\) consists of the squares \\((i,n)\\) with \\(i\\neq r\\).  A rectangle that\ncovers a square of column \\(n\\) must have its column interval contain \\(n\\).  Because\nthe hole \\((r,n)\\) is forbidden, such a rectangle cannot contain row \\(r\\); hence it\nlies entirely either above row \\(r\\) (rows \\(1,\\dots ,r-1\\)) or below row \\(r\\)\n(rows \\(r+1,\\dots ,n\\)).  If \\(1<r<n\\) both parts are non‑empty, so at least two\nrectangles are required.  If \\(r=1\\) or \\(r=n\\) only one part exists, and a single\nvertical rect