                "content": ".\n\n**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Structure of the uncovered squares.**  \n   Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares forms a permutation matrix: after a suitable renumbering of rows and columns the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) with \\(n=2025\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound (construction).**  \n   Tile each row \\(i\\) by a single horizontal rectangle covering all columns except the uncovered one in that row. This uses exactly one tile per row, i.e. \\(n\\) tiles, and clearly satisfies all conditions.\n\n3. **Lower bound (rank argument).**  \n   Represent the covering by a binary matrix \\(M\\) of size \\(n\\times n\\) where \\(M_{ij}=1\\) iff the square \\((i,j)\\) is covered. After the renumbering of step 1 we have  \n\n   \\[\n   M = J - I,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity.  \n   Each rectangular tile covering a set of rows \\(X\\) and columns \\(Y\\) corresponds to a rank‑1 matrix \\(u v^{\\!\\top}\\) (with \\(u\\) the characteristic vector of \\(X\\) and \\(v\\) that of \\(Y\\)).  \n   Since the tiles form a *partition* of the covered squares,  \n\n   \\[\n   M = \\sum_{k=1}^{t} u_k v_k^{\\!\\top},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(t\\) is the number of tiles.  \n   The rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks, and each summand has rank 1; hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{rank}(M) \\le t .\n   \\]\n\n   The matrix \\(J-I\\) has eigenvalues \\(n-1\\) (multiplicity 1) and \\(-1\\) (multiplicity \\(n-1\\)), so \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n\\). Consequently \\(t\\ge n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows \\(t\\le n\\) and the rank argument shows \\(t\\ge n\\); therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nConsider the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares; rows are indexed by \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns by \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nBy hypothesis each row contains exactly one uncovered square and each column contains exactly one uncovered square.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we permute the columns according to \\(\\pi^{-1}\\) (i.e. rename column \\(j\\) as \\(\\pi^{-1}(j)\\)), the set \\(U\\) becomes the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince a permutation of rows or columns is effected by multiplying the adjacency matrix on the left or right by a permutation matrix, all algebraic properties (in particular rank) are preserved.  Hence it suffices to work with the diagonal configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the set of all unit squares in row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\).  \nEach \\(R_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile, the tiles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct rows), and together they cover every square that is not in \\(U'\\).  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lower bound via matrix rank\n\nDefine the binary matrix \\(M\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times n}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nM_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if the square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered by a tile},\\\\[4pt]\n0 & \\text{if }(i,j)\\in U'.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAfter the column permutation of §1 we have  \n\n\\[\nM = J - I,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the \\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  Each tile yields a rank‑1 matrix\n\nLet a tile cover the set of rows \\(X\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(Y\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(X\\cap Y=\\varnothing\\) because a tile cannot contain a diagonal cell).  \nIts incidence matrix is  \n\n\\[\nA = \\mathbf{1}_X\\,\\mathbf{1}_Y^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{1}_X\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf{1}_Y\\)) is the column vector that has entry 1 exactly on the indices in \\(X\\) (resp. \\(Y\\)).  \nThus \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  The covering is a partition\n\nSince the tiles are disjoint and together cover all squares except the diagonal, the matrices of the tiles sum to \\(M\\):\n\n\\[\nM = \\sum_{k=1}^{t} A_k,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t\\) is the number of tiles and each \\(A_k\\) has rank 1.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.3  Rank inequality\n\nFor any matrices \\(B_1,\\dots ,B_t\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}\\!\\Bigl(\\sum_{k=1}^{t} B_k\\Bigr) \\le \\sum_{k=1}^{t} \\operatorname{rank}(B_k).\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the decomposition above gives\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M) \\le \\sum_{k=1}^{t} \\operatorname{rank}(A_k)=t.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.4  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\)\n\nThe eigenvalues of \\(J-I\\) are easily obtained.  \nThe vector \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\!\\top}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n(J-I)\\mathbf{1} = (n-1)\\mathbf{1},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\lambda_1=n-1\\) is an eigenvalue.  \nAny vector \\(v\\) with \\(\\mathbf{1}^{\\!\\top}v=0\\) satisfies \\(Jv=0\\), whence  \n\n\\[\n(J-I)v = -v,\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\lambda_2=-1\\) has multiplicity \\(n-1\\).  \nAll eigenvalues are non‑zero; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n.\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.5  Consequence\n\nFrom the inequality in §3.3 we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt \\ge \\operatorname{rank}(M)=n .\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling satisfying the problem’s conditions must use at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Minimality\n\nWe have shown\n\n* a construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles (Section 2), and\n* a universal lower bound of \\(n\\) tiles (Section 3).\n\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(n\\).\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
