ly \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nConsider the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares; rows are indexed by \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns by \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nBy hypothesis each row contains exactly one uncovered square and each column contains exactly one uncovered square.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we permute the columns according to \\(\\pi^{-1}\\) (i.e. rename column \\(j\\) as \\(\\pi^{-1}(j)\\)), the set \\(U\\) becomes the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince a permutation of rows or columns is effected by multiplying the adjacency matrix on the left or right by a permutation matrix, all algebraic properties (in particular rank) are preserved.  Hence it suffices to work with the diagonal configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the set of all unit squares in row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\).  \nEach \\(R_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile, the tiles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct rows), and together they cover every square that is not in \\(U'\\).  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lower bound via matrix rank\n\nDefine the binary matrix \\(M\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times n}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nM_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if the square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered by a tile},\\\\[4pt]\n0 & \\text{i