exactly \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the \\(n\\times n\\) grid by \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nBy hypothesis each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we permute the columns according to \\(\\pi^{-1}\\) (i.e. rename column \\(j\\) as \\(\\pi^{-1}(j)\\)), the set \\(U\\) becomes the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the incidence matrix of the covering on the right by a permutation matrix does not change its rank, so all algebraic arguments may be carried out for the diagonal configuration without loss of generality.\n\n#### 2.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the set of all unit squares in row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\).  \nEach \\(R_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile; the tiles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows, and together they cover every square not belonging to \\(U'\\).  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le n .\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound via matrix rank  \n\nDefine the binary matrix \\(M\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times n}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nM_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if the square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered},\\\\\n0 & \\text{if }(i,j)\\in U'.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAfter the column permutation of \u00a71 we have  \n\n\\[\nM = J - I,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the \\(n\\times n\\) all\u2011ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\n##### 3.1  Each tile contributes a rank\u20111 matrix  \n\nConsider a tile that covers the set of rows \\(X\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(Y\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nSince the tile must avoid the diagonal cells, we have \\(X\\cap Y=\\varnothing\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathbf{1}_X\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf{1}_Y\\)) denote the column vector of length \\(n\\) whose entries are \\(1\\) exactly on the indices belonging to \\(X\\) (resp. \\(Y\\)).  \nThe indicator matrix of the tile is then  \n\n\\[\nA = \\mathbf{1}_X\\,\\mathbf{1}_Y^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\nwhich has rank \\(1\\) because it is the outer product of two non\u2011zero vectors.\n\n##### 3.2  Decomposition of \\(M\\)  \n\nThe tiles are disjoint and together cover precisely the squares indicated by \\(M\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nM = \\sum_{k=1}^{t} A_k,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t\\) is the number of tiles and each \\(A_k\\) is a rank\u20111 matrix of the form described in \u00a73.1.\n\n##### 3.3  Rank inequality  \n\nFor any matrices \\(B_1,\\dots ,B_t\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}\\!\\Bigl(\\sum_{k=1}^{t} B_k\\Bigr)\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(B_k).\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the decomposition of \\(M\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(A_k)=t.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge \\operatorname{rank}(M).\n\\]\n\n##### 3.4  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\)  \n\nThe eigenvalues of \\(J-I\\) are easily obtained.  \nThe vector \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\!\\top}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n(J-I)\\mathbf{1} = (n-1)\\mathbf{1},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\lambda_1=n-1\\) is an eigenvalue.  \nAny vector \\(v\\) orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf{1}\\) (i.e. \\(\\mathbf{1}^{\\!\\top}v=0\\)) satisfies \\(Jv=0\\), whence  \n\n\\[\n(J-I)v = -v,\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\lambda_2=-1\\) has multiplicity \\(n-1\\).  \nAll eigenvalues are non\u2011zero; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n\\), and the inequality of \u00a73.3 gives  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge n.\n\\]\n\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles (Section\u202f2) and proved that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles (Section\u202f3).  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(n\\).\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\n- Verify that the \u201cPermutation of uncovered squares\u201d step correctly reduces the problem to the diagonal case without affecting the tile count or rank argument.  \n- Ensure the rank argument uses only valid properties: each tile\u2019s indicator matrix is rank\u202f1, the sum of disjoint indicator matrices equals the covering matrix, and the rank inequality \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A+B)\\le\\operatorname{rank}(A)+\\operatorname{rank}(B)\\) holds for any matrices.  \n- Confirm the eigenvalue computation for \\(J-I\\) is correct and that \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n\\).  \n- Check that the construction in Section\u202f2 indeed uses exactly one tile per row, covering all squares except the diagonal, and that tiles do not overlap.  \n- Ensure all mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters and the final answer is presented as \\(\\boxed{2025}\\).  \n- Confirm the output follows the required section order and headings exactly."
